   112	send a latin square~$A$ to itself, 
   113	$$A = \{(\rho r,\gamma c, \lambda l)\,:\,(r,c,l) \in A\},$$
   114	is called the \emph{autotopism group}
   115	of~$A$.
   116	The group of all paratopisms (i.e., quadruples 
   117	$(\rho,\gamma,\lambda,\pi)$ where $\pi$ is a permutation of $\{0,1,2\}$) that 
   118	send a latin square~$A$ to itself, 
   119	$$A = \{(\rho r,\gamma c, \lambda l)\,:\,(r,c,l) \in A_{\pi(0),\pi(1),\pi(2)}\},$$
   120	is called the \emph{paratopism group} of~$A$.
   121	Note that two latin squares are paratopic if and only if their graphs are isomorphic, 
   122	while this is not always true for $m\times n$ latin rectangles
   123	if $2m\le n$.
   124	
   125	
   126	We say that a latin  rectangle (square) $S$ is \emph{$K_{3,3}$-free} 
   127	if $\Gamma(S)$ has no induced subgraph isomorphic to $K_{3,3}$,
   128	the complete bipartite graph with two parts of size~$3$. (Recall that an \emph{induced} subgraph of a graph is another graph, formed from a subset of the vertices of the graph and all of the edges, from the original graph, connecting pairs of vertices in that subset.)
   129	It is easy to see the following proposition (a proof can be found in the appendix), which illustrates the fact that
   130	the {$K_{3,3}$-free} latin rectangles are the latin rectangles that avoid the six-element pattern
   131	 $$ \begin{array}{ccc}
   132	 \cdot & \mathbf C & \mathbf B \\
   133	  \mathbf C  & \cdot  & \mathbf A \\
   134	  \mathbf B  & \mathbf A & \cdot
   135	 \end{array}\qquad $$
   136	 in any three rows and three columns (the order of those rows and columns can be different from the order of the corresponding rows and columns in the pattern).
   137	\begin{proposition}\label{p:six}
   138	Six distinct elements $x$, $x'$, $x''$, $y$, $y'$, $y''$ of a latin rectangle $S$ induce a complete bipartite
   139	subgraph of \,$\Gamma(S)$ with parts $\{x, x', x''\}$ and $\{y, y', y''\}$ if and only if
   140	%  $x=(r_1,c_2,l_3)$,  $x'=(r_2,c_3,l_1)$,  $x''=(r_3,c_1,l_2)$, and
   141	\begin{align*}%\label{eq:123}
   142	 \{x,x',x''\}&=\{(r_1,c_2,l_3),(r_2,c_3,l_1),(r_3,c_1,l_2)\}
   143	 \mbox{ and }\\ %\label{eq:213}
   144	 \{y, y', y''\}&=\{(r_1,c_3,l_2), (r_2,c_1,l_3), (r_3,c_2,l_1)\}
   145	 \end{align*}
   146	for some $r_1$, $r_2$, $r_3$, $c_1$, $c_2$, $c_3$, $l_1$, $l_2$, $l_3$.
   147	\end{proposition}
