   181	
   182	The current paper is devoted to the following question,
   183	discussed at the open-problem section of
   184	the International Conference and PhD-Master Summer School on Graphs and Groups, Representations and Relations (August 2018, Novosibirsk).
   185	
   186	\begin{problem}\label{prob:1}
   187	 For which $n$ are there $K_{3,3}$-free latin squares of order~$n$?
   188	\end{problem}
   189	
   190	The following elementary proposition illustrates the problem, stating that the value tables of finite groups
   191	are not $K_{3,3}$-free (trivially, such a table is a latin square).
   192	A proof can be found in the appendix.
   193	
   194	\begin{proposition}\label{p:commut}
   195	 Given a finite group $(R,\cdot)$ of order at least~$3$, the value table $S=\{(r,c,r\cdot c)\mid r,c \in R\}$ is not $K_{3,3}$-free.
   196	\end{proposition}
   197	
   198	As for many other classes of combinatorial objects,
   199	the study of latin squares with or without certain patterns
   200	is a popular direction of research.
   201	The most popular patterns are latin subsquares
   202	(see, e.g., \cite{Heinrich:1980}, \cite{Heinrich:Ch4}, \cite{MWW:2007}, \cite{KSSS:2023}, \cite{AllsopWanless:subsquares}),
   203	trades (see the survey \cite{Cav:rev}),
   204	transversals
   205	(see the surveys \cite{Wanless:survey2011}, \cite{Montgomery:2024})
   206	and their multifold generalizations, plexes
   207	(see, e.g., \cite{EganWanless:2008}, \cite{Pula:2011}, \cite{Taranenko:2018:plex}).
   208	
   209	Problem~\ref{prob:1} has already been considered in~\cite{BroWan:2011}
   210	(in a different context 
   211	of universally noncommutative quasigroups,
   212	which is essentially the same 
   213	as $K_{3,3}$-free latin squares), 
   214	where it was established computationally 
   215	that for $3\le n\le 11$, there are only two main classes of such squares, both with $n=8$.
   216	In this paper, we repeat the calculation, paying more attention
   217	to intermediate results, in particular, to $K_{3,3}$-free latin rectangles,
   218	and make a step forward by constructing a $K_{3,3}$-free latin square
   219	of order~$16$.
   220	Additionally, we consider a similar problem for orthogonal latin squares,
   221	establishing the existence of an infinite number of $K_{4,4}$-free linear pairs of orthogonal latin squares, which contrasts with the case of linear latin squares
   222	(Proposition~\ref{p:commut}).
   223	
   224	
   225	In Section~\ref{s:motiv}, we show the place of Problem~1 in the general context of trades
   226	of combinatorial configurations, or, more specifically, of transversal trades in latin squares, and in the context of studying the minimum supports of eigenfunctions of graphs.
   227	In Section~\ref{s:9}, we report the results of an exhaustive search that
   228	repeats the results of~\cite{BroWan:2011} saying that among
   229	the latin squares of order from~$3$ to~$11$, 
   230	there are only two nonparatopic $K_{3,3}$-free latin squares, both of order~$8$.
   231	Moreover, we count the $K_{3,3}$-free latin $m\times n$ rectangles for all~$m$ and~$n$
   232	not more than~$11$ and, in particular, find that 
   233	$K_{3,3}$-free latin $(n-1)\times n$ rectangles are also very rare objects.
   234	Apart from the order~$8$, we have found one $4\times 5$ rectangle,
   235	one $8\times 9$ rectangle, both with nice cyclic structures,
   236	and (by an additional nonexhaustive search)
   237	a less symmetric $11\times 12$ latin rectangle avoiding $K_{3,3}$.
   238	In Section~\ref{s:16}, trying to generalize the $8\times 8$ examples,
   239	we construct a $K_{3,3}$-free latin $16\times 16$ square
   240	as a switched combination of two orthogonal $8\times 8$ latin squares.
   241	For every pair of orthogonal $8\times 8$ latin squares, an admissible switching
   242	to combine them into a $K_{3,3}$-free $16\times 16$ square is searched
   243	as a solution of a system of linear equations over GF$(2)$, and it is notable
   244	that only one pair of such squares (among almost twelve thousand) admits a solution.
   245	For every order larger than~$11$ and different from~$16$,
   246	the problem of the existence of a $K_{3,3}$-free latin square remains open.
   247	In Section~\ref{s:ort},
   248	we discuss a similar problem for orthogonal latin squares and prove (Corollary~\ref{cor:K44}) that
   249	in the class of linear pairs of orthogonal latin squares, there are an infinite number
   250	of both $K_{4,4}$-free and non-$K_{4,4}$-free pairs.
   251	
