   636	Both rectangles (see Fig.~\ref{t:9}) have some nice symmetries (isotopisms, paratopisms)
   637	and
   638	may potentially generalize to higher orders.
   639	In each case,
   640	the last row shown in Fig.~\ref{t:9} does not
   641	belong to the rectangle;
   642	it shows the unique completion to a latin square.
   643	This square is not $K_{3,3}$-free, but all $K_{3,3}$-patterns ($12$ patterns and $60$ patterns, respectively, for orders $5$ and~$9$) necessarily
   644	involve elements from the last row.
   645	Additionally, with a nonexhaustive search, we found two $K_{3,3}$-free latin $11\times 12$ rectangles, see Fig.~\ref{t:12};
   646	each of them has only $2$ autotopisms and $4$ paratopisms (actually the search approach was similar, but at each step we kept only rectangles with a nontrivial paratopism group).
   647	
   648	We also performed a search for $K_{3,3}$-free latin rectangles that avoid intercalates
   649	(an intercalate is a latin $2\times 2$ subsquare, which is one of the most famous substructures
   650	in latin squares; for latin squares of order larger than~$4$, intercalates are equivalent to size-$4$ maximal cliques in the latin square graph). The results of their classification are shown in Table~\ref{t:inter}; the largest such rectangle we found is shown in Fig.~\ref{t:12}.
