  1148	
  1149	In Fig.~\ref{t:K44}, we can see examples of $K_{4,4}$-patterns in linear pairs of orthogonal latin squares of order~$4$ and~$5$.
  1150	Note that there are two types of $K_{4,4}$ subgraphs in a graph of a pair of orthogonal latin squares.
  1151	To distinguish these two types, we define the color
  1152	of an edge as follows: in the graph of two orthogonal latin squares $G$ and $L$, a pair of adjacent vertices
  1153	$(r,c,g,l)$ and $(r',c',g',l')$
  1154	is assigned color $1$, $2$, $3$, or~$4$ if
  1155	$r=r'$, $c=c'$, $g=g'$, or $l=l'$, respectively.
  1156	In a $K_{4,4}$-subgraph, the edges of each color
  1157	form a $1$-factor;
  1158	the edges of any two colors
  1159	form a $2$-factor, which is an $8$-cycle or the union
  1160	of two $4$-cycles. We define the type of a $K_{4,4}$-subgraph as the number of two-color $8$-cycles in it.
  1161	\begin{lemma}
  1162	 There are only two possible types, type~$0$  and type~$4$.
  1163	\end{lemma}
  1164	Examples of type-0 and type-4 patterns can be found
  1165	in Fig.~\ref{t:K44} (the $4\times 4$ square and the two $5\times 5$ squares, respectively).
  1166	\begin{proof}
  1167	Let $P_0$ and $P_1$ be the bipartite parts of~$K_{4,4}$.
  1168	Straightforwardly,
  1169	the set $(r,c,l)$, where $r\in P_0$, $c\in P_1$,
  1170	and $l$ is the color of the edge $\{r,c\}$,
  1171	is a latin square of order~$4$.
  1172	The two isotopy classes
  1173	of latin squares of order~$4$
  1174	correspond to the two types of colorings, which can be checked directly.
  1175	\end{proof}
  1176	\begin{figure}
  1177	 $$
