   300	
   301	In the context described above, Problem~\ref{prob:1} can be viewed as a particular question concerning the study
   302	of the spectrum of volumes of transversal trades (or, more generally, clique trades).
   303	
   304	%====================================
   305	%====================================
   306	\subsection{Minimum supports of eigenfunctions}\label{s:eigen}
   307	
   308	An \emph{eigenfunction} corresponding to an \emph{eigenvalue} $\theta$ 
   309	of a graph $\Gamma=(V,E)$ is a real-valued function
   310	$f:V\to\mathbb{R}$ such that $f\not\equiv 0$ and
   311	$\sum_{y:\{x,y\}\in E} f(y)=\theta  f(x)$ 
   312	for all~$x$ from~$V$.
   313	Many classes of combinatorial configurations and their trades are representable
   314	as functions with a pure eigenspectrum; often (including all examples mentioned in Section~\ref{s:trades}),
   315	as eigenfunctions satisfying additional discrete constraints on their values.
   316	In this context, the study of the smallest volume of trades motivates the study
   317	of the smallest support of eigenfunctions, for given graph and eigenvalue.
   318	For different graphs, this question was considered in
   319	\cite{KMP:16:trades},
   320	\cite{Sotnikova:2018}, \cite{Val:min2}, \cite{VorVal:Hamm}, \cite{VMV:Johnson}, \cite{GKSV:Paley},
   321	\cite{Sotnikova:bil}, \cite{SotVal:survey2021}, \cite{Adriaensen:wd}.
   322	
   323	The general results of \cite{KMP:16:trades} for distance-regular graphs, applied to a latin square graph, imply the following.
   324	\begin{proposition}\label{p:6}
   325	 An eigenfunction corresponding to the {eigenvalue} $-3$ of a latin square graph has at least $6$ nonzeros. Moreover, 
   326	 if the number of nonzeros  is exactly $6$, 
   327	 then they induce a subgraph 
   328	 isomorphic to $K_{3,3}$.
   329	\end{proposition}
   330	
   331	So, Problem 1 is a special case of the minimum-support problem for eigenfunctions of graphs.
   332	Moreover, in the case of strongly regular graphs,
   333	the eigenfunctions with the number of nonzeros attaining the so-called
   334	weight-distribution lower bound correspond to induced $K_{s,s}$-subgraphs,
   335	where $s$ is derived from the parameters of the strongly regular graph and the eigenvalue. In a sequence of papers
   336	\cite{GKSV:Paley,GShY:2023,GorPan:2024,GorYip:2024,DeBruyn2025,EvGoSh:2025,Adriaensen:wd}, it is proved that the weight-distribution bound is attained for different families of strongly regular graphs (in the recent work \cite{Adriaensen:wd}, also the non-attainability of this bound for some graphs is proved). $K_{3,3}$-free latin squares and $K_{4,4}$-free pairs of orthogonal latin squares
   337	(constructed in Section~\ref{s:ort}) give examples of strongly regular graphs
   338	whose eigenfunctions do not attain the weight-distribution lower bound on the cardinality of the support.
   339	
   340	\begin{remark}
   341	 If one treats an eigenspace of a graph as a linear code,
   342	then the minimum size of the support of an eigenfunction from this space 
   343	is the minimum Hamming distance of this code.
   344	%[!!!!! mb try to treat K33-free over finite fields]
   345	\end{remark}
   346	
   347	%====================================
   348	%====================================
   349	%====================================
   350	\section{Small orders}\label{s:9}
   351	
   352	In this section, we report the results of a computer search for $K_{3,3}$-free latin rectangles
   353	of order at most~$11$.
   354	
   355	We use the standard algorithm of classification of latin rectangles, see e.g. \cite{KO:alg}, 
   356	adapted to the $K_{3,3}$-free case.
   357	We first describe the general approach, without the $K_{3,3}$-free condition.
   358	The algorithm is recursive in~$m$;
   359	for each given $m\times n$ latin rectangle $S_{m,n}\subset R_m\times C \times L$,  
   360	it produces all possible continuations to a latin rectangle of size $(m+1)\times n$.
   361	\begin{itemize}
   362	\item[(i)] Let $m$ be the new row index, $m\not\in R_m$. 
   363	 Among all $n^2$ elements $(m,c,l)$, $c\in C$, $l\in L$,
   364	 we choose $(n-m)n$ elements $(m,c,l)$, \emph{candidates}, such that 
   365	 $S_{m,n}$ does not contain any triple $(r,c,l)$, $r\in R_m$.
   366	\item[(ii)] On the set of candidates, we define the compatibility graph $\Delta$ as follows.
   367	 Two vertices $(m,c',l')$, $(m,c'',l'')$ are compatible (i.e., adjacent in $\Delta$)
   368	 if and only if $c'\ne c''$ and $l'\ne l''$.
   369	\item[(iii)] Using specialized software
   370	(e.g., \texttt{cliquer} \cite{cliquer})
   371	 we find all cliques~$Q$ of size~$n$ in the graph~$\Delta$.
   372	 The unions $S_{m,n}\cup Q$ are all possible $(m+1)\times n$
   373	 latin rectangles that continue $S_{m,n}$.
   374	\item[(iv)] Using the software \cite{nauty2014} for dealing with graph isomorphism,
   375	we find all nonparatopic representatives of the found latin rectangles, to use them at the next iterative step or to represent the result of the classification.
   376	\end{itemize}
   377	It happens that this approach can be easily adapted to classify the $K_{3,3}$-free latin rectangles.
   378	Indeed, if six elements of a latin rectangle $S_{m+1,n}$ form a forbidden pattern 
   379	(i.e., induce $K_{3,3}$ in $\Gamma(S_{m+1,n})$), then either they all lie in the first $m$ rows, 
   380	or exactly two of them belong to the last row.
   381	The first possibility is excluded if the latin rectangle $S_{m,n}$, constructed at the previous iteration, is $K_{3,3}$-free.
   382	To avoid the second possibility, we forbid the corresponding pairs in the last row by excluding them from the edge set of~$\Delta$.
   383	So, we replace~(ii) by
   384	\begin{itemize}
   385	\item[(ii')] On the set of candidates, we define the compatibility graph~$\Delta$ as follows.
   386	 Two vertices $(m,c',l')$, $(m,c'',l'')$ are compatible (i.e., adjacent in~$\Delta$)
   387	 if and only if $c'\ne c''$, $l'\ne l''$,
   388	 \emph{and, moreover,  
   389	 there are no $c$, $r'$, $r''$
   390	 such that $(r',c,l'),(r'',c,l'')\in S_{m,n}$
   391	 and $S_{m,n}(r',c'')=S_{m,n}(r'',c')$.}
   392	\end{itemize}
   393	Applying (i)-(ii')-(iii)-(iv) to representatives
   394	$S_{m,n}$ of the main classes of
   395	$K_{3,3}$-free  $m\times n$ latin rectangles,
   396	we obtain representatives of all main classes
   397	of
   398	$K_{3,3}$-free $(m+1)\times n$ latin rectangles.
   399	If the resulting set is nonempty, the standard approach \cite[10.2]{KO:alg}
   400	based on the orbit-stabilizer 
   401	theorem is applied to validate the results.
   402	The approach is based on the fact that the total number of latin rectangles
   403	from the considered class (see Table~\ref{t:3-10}) can be found in two ways:
   404	firstly, using the total number of solutions found at step~(iii)
   405	and the orders of the paratopism groups of the representatives~$S_{m,n}$;
   406	and secondly, using only nonparatopic solutions (step~(iv))
   407	and the orders of their paratopism groups.
   408	Many kinds of random or systematic errors in the calculation result in
   409	the disagreement in the values found with these two ways; 
   410	conversely, agreement between these values indicates
   411	that the probability of an error is convincingly low.
   412	
   413	Table~\ref{t:3-9} shows the number of nonparatopic
   414	$K_{3,3}$-free latin rectangles for small values of~$n$.
