   748	\caption{An example of the $0$-combination and a switched combination of two orthogonal latin $4\times 4$ squares, where switching destroys a $K_{3,3}$-pattern  }
   749	\label{t:ort4}
   750	\end{figure}
   751	
   752	Assume we have two $n\times n$ latin squares
   753	$A_0$ and~$A_1$
   754	and a $\{0,1\}$-valued array~$S$ of size $n\times n$, called a
   755	\emph{switching matrix}.
   756	We assume that $A_0$ and~$A_1$ have the same sets of rows and columns $\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}$ but disjoint sets of letters,
   757	say,
   758	$\{0,2,\ldots,2n-2\}$ and $\{1,3,\ldots,2n-1\}$.
   759	We define a $2n\times 2n$ latin square~$L$, called a
   760	\emph{switched combination} of~$A_0$
   761	and~$A_1$ (if $S$ is all-zero, just a \emph{$0$-combination}),
   762	in the following way: 
   763	\def\div#1#2{{#1/\!\!/#2}}
   764	\begin{equation}\label{eq:Lij}
   765	 L(r,c)=
   766	A_{r\oplus c\oplus S(\div{r}{2},\div{c}{2})}(\div{r}{2},\div{c}{2}),
   767	\end{equation}
   768	where $\oplus$ is the addition modulo~$2$ and $\div{}{}$ is the integer division.
   769	The construction is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{t:ort4}
   770	where the first square of order~$8$ is the combination of the two latin squares of order~$4$ with  the all-zero switching matrix, while the second
   771	square of order~$8$ is the combination with the switching matrix that has only one~$1$
   772	in the left top position.
   773	For the combined latin square, we refer to the set of four cells of the form $(2i,2j)$, $(2i,2j+1)$, $(2i+1,2j)$, $(2i+1,2j+1)$ as a \emph{block}.
   774	 
   775	\begin{lemma}
   776	 For a switched combination of two orthogonal latin squares, 
   777	 a forbidden pattern cannot be located in fewer than~$6$ blocks.
   778	 Conversely, a switched combination of two nonorthogonal latin squares
   779	 always contains a forbidden pattern located in $3$ blocks.
   780	\end{lemma}
   781	\begin{proof}
   782	 If a forbidden pattern is located in fewer than~$6$ blocks,
   783	 then one block contains two elements of it. Those two elements 
   784	 can be two cells with the same letter or two cells with different letters but in the same row or column.
   785	 In both cases, it is easy to see that the pattern is actually located in three blocks
   786	 $$
   787	      \begin{array}{|@{\ }c@{\ }c@{\ }|@{\ }c@{\ }c@{\ }|}
   788	\hline
   789	          \cdot &  A &  B & \cdot  \\
   790	          A &  \cdot &  C & \cdot  \\  
   791	\hline
   792	          \cdot & \cdot &  \cdot & \cdot  \\ 
   793	          B & C &  \cdot & \cdot  \\
   794	\hline
   795	   \end{array}
   796	$$
   797	and there are two blocks that contain the same pair of letters. 
   798	This contradicts the definition of orthogonality and proves the first claim.
   799	
   800	The second claim is simple to see, as a switched combination of two nonorthogonal
   801	latin squares contains two blocks with the same pair of letters and hence contains
   802	a forbidden pattern as above.
   803	\end{proof}
   804	
   805	So, if we want to get a $K_{3,3}$-free switched combination, then the combined latin squares
   806	must be orthogonal and in this case it suffices to avoid forbidden pattern with 
   807	$6$ elements in $6$ distinct blocks.
   808	In the first $8 \times 8$ latin square in Fig.~\ref{t:ort4}, 
   809	a pattern is indicated by six bold letters.
   810	In the second $8 \times 8$ latin square, we switched one of the six blocks
   811	containing that pattern and see that those six blocks do not contain a forbidden pattern anymore.
   812	\begin{lemma} {\rm(i)}
   813	 If a switched combination of two latin squares contains a forbidden pattern in $6$ blocks,
   814	 then the switching matrix~$S$ has an even number of ones in the positions corresponding
   815	 to those $6$ blocks.
   816	  {\rm(ii)} Moreover, any other switching matrix with an even number of ones in those six positions
   817	 gives (by combining the same two latin squares) a latin square
   818	 with a forbidden pattern in the corresponding six blocks.
   819	\end{lemma}
   820	\begin{proof}
   821	By Proposition~\ref{p:six}, we can assume that the six cells in a forbidden pattern are
   822	 $x=(r_1,c_2,l_3)$,  $x'=(r_2,c_3,l_1)$,  $x''=(r_3,c_1,l_2)$,
   823	$y=(r_2,c_1,l_3)$, $y'=(r_3,c_2,l_1)$, $y''=(r_1,c_3,l_2)$.
   824	
   825	 (i)  Consider the cells $x$ and $y$, which have the same letter~$l_3$.
   826	 From~\eqref{eq:Lij}, we see that
   827	 $ r_1\oplus c_2\oplus S(\div{r_1}{2},\div{c_2}{2})$ equals $ r_2\oplus c_1\oplus S(\div{r_2}{2},\div{c_1}{2} )$ (which is $0$ if $l_3$ is even and $1$ if $l_3$ is odd). So, we have
   828	 \begin{equation}\label{eq:xy}
   829	  r_1\oplus c_2 \oplus r_2\oplus c_1 = S(\div{r_1}{2},\div{c_2}{2})\oplus S(\div{r_2}{2},\div{c_1}{2}).
   830	 \end{equation}
   831	 Considering similarly $x'$ and $y'$ (with letter~$l_1$), then $x''$ and $y''$ (with letter~$l_2$), we get
   832	 \begin{equation}\label{eq:xy'}
   833	  r_2\oplus c_3 \oplus r_3\oplus c_2
   834	  =
   835	  S(\div{r_2}{2},\div{c_3}{2}) \oplus S(\div{r_3}{2},\div{c_2}{2})
   836	 \end{equation}
   837	 and
   838	 \begin{equation}\label{eq:xy''}
   839	  r_3\oplus c_1 \oplus r_1\oplus c_3
   840	  =
   841	  S(\div{r_3}{2},\div{c_1}{2}) \oplus S(\div{r_1}{2},\div{c_3}{2}).
   842	 \end{equation}
   843	 Summing the three equations above, we get zero in the left side (indeed, each value is included twice) and the sum of
   844	 the six values of~$S$ in the right side, which proves that the number of nonzero values among this six is even.
   845	
   846	 (ii) Now, assume we have another switching matrix $\tilde S$
   847	 that switches an even number of blocks among the six blocks
   848	 containing $x$, $x'$, $x''$, $y$, $y'$, $y''$.
   849	 In the new combined latin square $\tilde L$,
   850	 constructed as the $\tilde S$-switched combination of the same two latin squares of order~$n$,
   851	 we consider the following six elements, expecting that they will form a forbidden pattern in the same six cells and with the same letters as $x$, $x'$, $x''$, $y$, $y'$, $y''$:
   852	 \begin{itemize}
   853	  \item
   854	  $\tilde x=(\tilde r_1,\tilde c_2,l_3)$ is any
   855	 of the two elements of $\tilde L$
   856	 in the block containing~$x$ (in~$L$)
   857	 that have the letter~$l_3$ (the letter of~$x$).
   858	 \item
   859	 $\tilde y'=(\tilde r_3,\tilde c_2,l_1)$ is the element of $\tilde L$ lying in the same block as~$y'$
   860	 and in the same column $\tilde c_2$ as~$\tilde x$ and having
   861	 the letter~$l_1$ (the letter of~$y'$).
   862	 \item
   863	 $\tilde x''=(\tilde r_3,\tilde c_1,l_2)$
   864	 is the element of $\tilde L$ lying in the same block as~$x''$
   865	 and the same row $\tilde r_3$ as~$\tilde y'$ and having
   866	 the letter~$l_2$ (the letter of~$x'$).
   867	 \item
   868	 $\tilde y=(\tilde r_2,\tilde c_1,l_3)$ is the element of $\tilde L$ lying in the same block as~$y$
   869	 and the same column $\tilde c_1$ as~$\tilde x''$ and having
   870	 the letter~$l_3$ (the letter of~$y$).
   871	 \item
   872	 $\tilde x'=(\tilde r_2,\tilde c_3,l_1)$
   873	 is the element of $\tilde L$ lying in the same block as~$x'$
   874	 and the same row $\tilde r_3$ as~$\tilde y$ and having
   875	 the letter~$l_1$ (the letter of~$x'$).
   876	 \item Finally,
   877	 $\tilde y''=(\tilde r_1,\tilde c_3,\tilde l_2)$
   878	 is the element of~$\tilde L$
   879	 in the same column~$\tilde c_3$ as~$\tilde x'$ and
   880	 in the same row $\tilde r_1$ as~$\tilde x$.
   881	
   882	 \end{itemize}
   883	To see that these six elements of $\tilde L$
   884	form a forbidden pattern, it remains to ensure
   885	that $\tilde l_2 = l_2$. Assuming the contrary, we have that
   886	$\tilde x''$ and $\tilde y''$ have different letters,
   887	while $\tilde x$ and $\tilde y$ have equal letters,
   888	and $\tilde x'$ and $\tilde y'$ have equal letters.
   889	Similarly to~\eqref{eq:xy}--\eqref{eq:xy''}, from~\eqref{eq:Lij}
   890	we derive
   891	 \begin{eqnarray*}\label{eq:txy''}
   892	  \tilde r_3\oplus \tilde c_1 \oplus \tilde r_1\oplus \tilde  c_3
   893	  &\!\!\not =\!\!&
   894	  \tilde S(\div{\tilde r_3}{2},\div{\tilde c_1}{2}) \oplus \tilde S(\div{\tilde r_1}{2},\div{\tilde c_3}{2}),
   895	  \\
   896	    \tilde r_1\oplus \tilde c_2 \oplus \tilde r_2\oplus \tilde c_1
   897	    &\!\!=\!\!&
   898	    \tilde S(\div{\tilde r_1}{2},\div{\tilde c_2}{2})\oplus \tilde S(\div{\tilde r_2}{2},\div{\tilde c_1}{2}),
   899	    \\
   900	  \tilde r_2\oplus \tilde c_3 \oplus \tilde r_3\oplus \tilde c_2
   901	  &\!\!=\!\!&
   902	  \tilde S(\div{\tilde r_2}{2},\div{\tilde c_3}{2}) \oplus \tilde S(\div{\tilde r_3}{2},\div{\tilde c_2}{2}).
   903	 \end{eqnarray*}
   904	 Summing these three (in)equations with~$\oplus$,
   905	 we get $0$ in the left (each value repeats twice), $0$ in the right (even number of switches), and $\not=$ in between,
   906	 a~contradiction. Hence, $\tilde l_2 = l_2$ and the six elements $\tilde x$, $\tilde x'$, $\tilde x''$, $\tilde y$, $\tilde y'$, $\tilde y''$ form a forbidden pattern.
   907	\end{proof}
   908	
   909	
   910	
   911	
   912	
   913	
   914	
   915	
   916	
   917	
   918	
   919	
   920	
   921	
   922	
   923	
   924	
   925	
   926	Now, the strategy is as follows. We take a pair of orthogonal latin squares $A_0$, $A_1$
   927	of order~$n$
   928	and consider the elements $s_{i,j}$, $i,j=0,\ldots,n-1$ 
   929	of an $n\times n$ switching matrix as variables.
   930	We construct the $0$-combination $L_0$ of~$A_0$ and~$A_1$ and locate all forbidden patterns.
   931	For a forbidden pattern in blocks with coordinates 
   932	$(i  ,j'')$,
   933	$(i' ,j'')$,
   934	$(i  ,j  )$,
   935	$(i' ,j' )$,
   936	$(i'',j  )$,
   937	$(i'',j' )$,
   938	we consider the equation
   939	$$ 
   940	s_{i  ,j''} +
   941	s_{i' ,j''} +
   942	s_{i  ,j  } +
   943	s_{i' ,j' } +
   944	s_{i'',j  } +
   945	s_{i'',j' }  = 1
   946	$$
   947	over GF$(2)$. All such equations, for all forbidden patterns in~$L_0$,
   948	form a system of linear equations. If the system has a solution,
   949	then a $K_{3,3}$-free latin square is constructed as the combination 
   950	             of~$A_0$ and~$A_1$ with switching matrix from the solution.
   951	             
   952	As was found in \cite{EganWanless:2016}, there are $11887$ pairs of orthogonal $8\times 8$
   953	latin squares, up to the following equivalence:
   954	for our approach, the pairs of orthogonal latin squares are essentially the same
   955	if one pair can be obtained from the other by isotopy,
   956	swapping the roles of rows and columns, and/or swapping the two orthogonal squares
   957	(however, for example, changing the roles of columns and letters of the first square
   958	can result in the essentially different combination).
   959	  Representatives of the $11887$ equivalence classes
   960	  with respect to this equivalence
   961	  can be found from representatives of the 
   962	  $2165$ main classes of pairs of orthogonal $8\times 8$
   963	latin squares~\cite{MK:data}.
   964	Finally, we have found that one (and only one of~$11887$) pair of orthogonal latin squares
   965	yields a solvable system, see Fig.~\ref{t:888}. There are $2^{15}$ solutions
   966	(Fig.~\ref{t:888} shows only one of them, the other can be obtained
   967	by changing all the values in some columns and rows). They result
   968	in isotopic $K_{3,3}$-free latin squares of order~$16$; one of them is shown in 
   969	Fig.~\ref{t:16}.
   970	The found latin square has only $32$ autotopisms (the full paratopism group has order~$64$),
   971	as well as the corresponding $0$-combination. In particular, the paratopism group
   972	does not act transitively on cells (equivalently, the square is not isotopic to a G-loop),
   973	in contrast to the $K_{3,3}$-free latin squares of order~$8$.
   974	Each of the two combined $8\times 8$
   975	latin squares is highly symmetric:
   976	the autotopism group acts transitively on the $64$ cells
   977	(that is, we have a G-loop isotope) and has order
   978	$64$ and $192$, respectively. However, the autotopism group
   979	of the pair of orthogonal latin squares has order~$16$ only.
   980	The first of the combined $8\times 8$ squares (Fig.~\ref{t:888})
   981	is itself a switched combination of the $4\times 4$
   982	squares from Fig.~\ref{t:8}; notably,
   983	the switching matrix is the mod-$2$ sum of
   984	the switching matrices for the two $K_{3,3}$-free
   985	squares in Fig.~\ref{t:8}
   986	(see the grey cells in Fig.~\ref{t:8} and Fig.~\ref{t:888}).
   987	The structure of the second
   988	square in Fig.~\ref{t:888} is less understood.
   989	
   990	
   991	\begin{problem}
   992	 What is so special in those two orthogonal latin squares (Fig.~\ref{t:888})?
   993	 Can the construction of $K_{3,3}$-free (universally noncommutative) latin squares
   994	 be generalized to higher orders?
   995	\end{problem}
