  1092	\section{\texorpdfstring{$K_{4,4}$}{K44}-free pairs of orthogonal latin squares}\label{s:ort}
  1093	The problem of the existence of $K_{3,3}$-free latin squares
  1094	can be naturally generalized to sets of mutually orthogonal latin squares.
  1095	% Two latin squares $S\subset R\times C\times L$ and $S'\subset R\times C\times L'$ of the same order are called \emph{orthogonal} if $S(x,y)=S(u,v)$ and $S'(x,y)=S'(u,v)$ imply $(x,y)=(u,v)$.
  1096	It is known that a set of $k$, $1\le k \le n-2$, mutually orthogonal latin squares (MOLS) of order~$n$
  1097	corresponds to a
  1098	% diameter-$2$ distance-regular
  1099	strongly regular
  1100	graph.
  1101	According to \cite[Theorem~3]{KMP:16:trades}, an eigenfunction of this graph corresponding to the eigenvalue $-k-2$ has the minimum number $2(k+2)$ of nonzeros only if these nonzeros
  1102	induce a subgraph isomorphic to $K_{k+2,k+2}$.
  1103	The study of $K_{k+2,k+2}$-free collections of $k$ MOLS is nontrivial even in the linear case,
  1104	when $n$ is a prime power and all latin squares are linear functions
  1105	% $S(r,c)=\alpha r +\beta c$
  1106	over GF$(n)$:
  1107	among the linear pairs $(G,L)$ of orthogonal latin squares over GF$(n)$, where $n\ge 7$ is an odd prime power,
  1108	both $K_{4,4}$-free and non-$K_{4,4}$-free pairs exist, see Theorem~\ref{th:K44nK44} below.
  1109	
  1110	We first adapt the terminology to pairs of orthogonal latin squares.
  1111	Such a pair $(A,B)$ of order~$n$ will also be treated as the set $\{(r,c,G(r,c),L(r,c)):\ c\in C, r\in R \}$ of ordered quadruples;
  1112	the graph $\Gamma(A,B)$ of this pair has this set as the vertex set, two quadruples being adjacent
  1113	if they coincide in one position.
  1114	A set of $8$ elements of a pair $(A,B)$ of orthogonal latin squares is called
  1115	a $K_{4,4}$-pattern if it induces a subgraph of $\Gamma(A,B)$ isomorphic to $K_{4,4}$. If no such patterns exist, the pair $(A,B)$ is called  \emph{$K_{4,4}$-free}.
  1116	
  1117	Two pairs of orthogonal latin squares
  1118	$(A,B)\subset {R} \times {C} \times {G} \times {L}$ and
  1119	$(A',B')\subset {R'} \times {C'} \times {G'} \times {L'}$ are \emph{isotopic} if there are bijections $\rho:R\to R'$, $\gamma:C\to C'$, $\delta:G\to G'$, and $\lambda:L\to L'$
  1120	such that
  1121	$(A',B') = \{(\rho r,\gamma c, \delta g, \lambda l):\ (r,c,g,l) \in (A,B)\}$.
  1122	If
  1123	$(A',B') = \{\pi(\rho r,\gamma c, \delta g, \lambda l):\ (r,c,g,l) \in (A,B)\}$,
  1124	where $\pi$ is one of $4!$ permutations of coordinates,
  1125	then $(A,B)$ and $(A',B')$ are called \emph{paratopic}.
  1126	
  1127	
  1128	
