  1209	\begin{theorem}\label{th:K44nK44}
  1210	 Let $G,L_\kappa:\mathrm{GF}(q)^2\to \mathrm{GF}(q)$
  1211	 be two orthogonal latin squares of order~$q$,
  1212	 where $q$ is a prime power,
  1213	 \begin{equation*}\label{eq:GLk}
  1214	   G(r,c)=r+c,\quad
  1215	 L_\kappa(r,c)=r+\kappa c,\qquad \kappa \in \mathrm{GF}(q)\setminus \{0,1\}.
  1216	 \end{equation*}
  1217	 \begin{itemize}
  1218	  \item[\rm(i)] The pair $(G,L_\kappa)$ contains a forbidden pattern
  1219	 of type~$4$ if and only if $q$ is odd and $\kappa\in\{2,1/2,-1\}$.
  1220	  \item[\rm(ii)] The pair $(G,L_\kappa)$ contains a forbidden pattern
  1221	 of type~$0$ if and only if $q$ is even.
  1222	 \end{itemize}
  1223	\end{theorem}
  1224	
  1225	\begin{proof} (i) \emph{Only if.}
  1226	We first consider the case when
  1227	\begin{itemize}
  1228	 \item[(a)] in the $K_{4,4}$-subgraph,
  1229	the edges of colors $1$ and~$2$ form two $4$-cycles and the edges of colors $3$ and~$4$ form two $4$-cycles in the $K_{4,4}$-subgraph;
  1230	 \item[(b)] the edges corresponding to any other pair of colors ($1$ and~$3$, $1$ and~$4$, $2$ and~$3$, $2$ and~$4$) form an $8$-cycle in the $K_{4,4}$-subgraph.
  1231	\end{itemize}
  1232	We are going to show that the pattern is similar to the one in the right square in Fig.~\ref{t:K44}, which illustrates the considerations below.
  1233	 Let the two $4$-cycles formed by edges of colors
  1234	 $1$ and $2$ be $v_{00}{-}v_{10}{-}v_{11}{-}v_{01}{-}v_{00}$ and
  1235	 $v_{22}{-}v_{32}{-}v_{33}{-}v_{23}{-}v_{22}$, where
  1236	$$
  1237	 \begin{array}{c@{}cc@{}c}
  1238	 &v_{22}=(r_2,c_2,\beta',E'), & v_{23}=(r_2,c_3,\gamma',D'), & \\
  1239	 v_{00}=(r_0,c_0,\beta,B), &&&
  1240	 v_{01}=(r_0,c_1,\varepsilon,D), \\
  1241	 v_{10}=(r_1,c_0,\gamma,C), &&&
  1242	 v_{11}=(r_1,c_1,\alpha,E), \\
  1243	 &v_{32}=(r_3,c_2,\varepsilon',C'), & v_{33}=(r_3,c_3,\alpha',B'). & \\
  1244	 \end{array}
  1245	$$
  1246	Obviously,
  1247	 $r_0$, $r_1$, $r_2$, $r_3$ are pairwise different;
  1248	 $c_0$, $c_1$, $c_2$, $c_3$ are pairwise different;
  1249	 $\beta$, $\gamma$, $\varepsilon$, $\alpha$ are pairwise different
  1250	 and $\{\beta$, $\gamma$, $\varepsilon$, $\alpha\}=\{\beta'$, $\varepsilon'$, $\gamma'$, $\alpha'\}$;
  1251	 $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$ are pairwise different
  1252	 and $\{B$, $C$, $D$, $E\}=\{E'$, $C'$, $D'$, $B'\}$.
  1253	
  1254	 Without loss of generality, we assume $\beta=\beta'$,
  1255	 which means that the bipartite parts of $K_{4,4}$ are
  1256	 $\{v_{00},v_{11},v_{23},v_{32}\}$ and $\{v_{01},v_{10},v_{22},v_{33}\}$. Now, if $\gamma=\varepsilon'$, then we have a $4$-cycle
  1257	 $v_{00}{-}v_{10}{-}v_{32}{-}v_{22}{-}v_{00}$ with colors $2$ and~$3$ of the edges, which contradicts condition~(b) above. Hence,
  1258	 $\gamma\not=\varepsilon'$ and $C=C'$. Similarly, considering the $4$-cycles
  1259	$v_{00}{-}v_{01}{-}v_{23}{-}v_{22}{-}v_{00}$ and
  1260	$v_{01}{-}v_{11}{-}v_{33}{-}v_{23}{-}v_{01}$,
  1261	we get $D=D'$ and $\alpha=\alpha'$.
  1262	
  1263	Now, since $\beta=\beta'$, $\gamma\not=\varepsilon'$, $\alpha=\alpha'$,  and
  1264	$\{\beta$, $\gamma$, $\varepsilon$, $\alpha\}=\{\beta'$, $\varepsilon'$, $\gamma'$, $\alpha'\}$, we have also $\gamma=\gamma'$
  1265	and $\varepsilon=\varepsilon'$. Similarly, $B=B'$ and $E=E'$.
  1266	
  1267	From $G(r_0,c_0)=G(r_2,c_2)$ $(=\beta)$, we have
  1268	$r_0+c_0 = r_2+c_2$; from $G(r_1,c_0)=G(r_2,c_3)$ $(=\gamma)$, we have
  1269	$r_1+c_0 = r_2+c_3$. We derive
  1270	\begin{equation}\label{eq:1032}
  1271	 r_0-r_1 = c_2-c_3.
  1272	\end{equation}
  1273	On the other hand,
  1274	from $L_\kappa(r_0,c_0)=L_\kappa(r_3,c_3)$ $(=B)$
  1275	we have
  1276	$r_0+\kappa c_0 = r_3+\kappa c_3$;
  1277	from $L_\kappa(r_1,c_0)=L_\kappa(r_3,c_2)$ $(=C)$
  1278	we have
  1279	$r_1+\kappa c_0 = r_3+\kappa c_2$.
  1280	We derive
  1281	\begin{equation}\label{eq:1023}
  1282	 r_0-r_1 = \kappa (c_3-c_2).
  1283	\end{equation}
  1284	From \eqref{eq:1032} and \eqref{eq:1023}, we find $\kappa = -1$.
  1285	
  1286	Now assume that, instead of (a) and (b), we have that the pairs of colors inducing two $4$-cycles in the $K_{4,4}$-subgraph
  1287	are $(1,3)$ and $(2,4)$. Since
  1288	$$
  1289	\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
  1290	 g=r+c\\
  1291	 l=r+\kappa c
  1292	\end{array}\right.
  1293	\quad\mbox{is equivalent to }
  1294	\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
  1295	 r=l+(-\kappa)c\\
  1296	 g=l+(1-\kappa) c
  1297	\end{array}\right. ,
  1298	$$
  1299	the pair $(G,L_\kappa)$ is paratopic to
  1300	$(G,L_{\kappa'})$ with $\kappa'=\frac{1-\kappa}{-\kappa}=1-\frac{1}{\kappa}$, where $(G,L_{\kappa'})$ satisfies (a) and~(b). We conclude $\kappa'=-1$ and $\kappa=2$.
  1301	
  1302	Similarly, the remaining case ($4$-cycles corresponding to the pairs of colors $(1,4)$ and $(2,3)$) leads to $\kappa=1/2$. It remains to note that in all the cases, even~$q$ leads to a contradiction ($\kappa=-1 \equiv 1 \bmod 2$ and $\kappa=2 \equiv 0 \bmod 2$ contradict the definition of orthogonal latin squares, $\kappa=1/2$ does not exist).
  1303	
  1304	(i) \emph{If.}
  1305	In the case $\kappa=-1$, the following eight vertices induce $K_{4,4}$:
  1306	 $$
  1307	 \begin{array}{cccc}
  1308	 &(0,1,1,-1), &
  1309	  (0,1{+}x,1{+}x,-1{-}x), & \\
  1310	 \makebox[1mm][c]{$(1,0,1,1)$,} &&&
  1311	 \makebox[1mm][c]{$(1,2{+}x,3{+}x,-1{-}x),$} \\
  1312	 \makebox[1mm][c]{$(1{+}x,0,1{+}x,1{+}x)$,} &&&
  1313	 \makebox[1mm][c]{$(1{+}x,2{+}x,3{+}2x,-1),$} \\
  1314	 & (2{+}x,1,3{+}x,1{+}x), &
  1315	 (2{+}x,1{+}x,3{+}2x,1), &
  1316	 \end{array}
  1317	$$ where $0$, $1$, $1+x$, $2+x$ are mutually different (for example, $x=1$ if the prime divisor of~$q$ is larger than~$3$ and $x\in\mathrm{GF}(q)\backslash\{0,1,2\}$ if $3$ divides~$q$).
  1318	From this example, using the paratopisms described at the end of the ``only if'' part of the proof, we obtain examples for $\kappa=2$ and $\kappa=1/2$.
  1319	
  1320	(ii) \emph{Only if.}
  1321	Instead of (a) and (b), we now have
  1322	\begin{itemize}
  1323	 \item[(c)]
  1324	the edges of any two colors from $1$, $2$, $3$, $4$ form two $4$-cycles in the $K_{4,4}$-subgraph.
  1325	\end{itemize}
  1326	Defining $v_{00}$, \ldots, $v_{33}$ as before,
  1327	we now derive from~(c)
  1328	that $\varepsilon'=\gamma$,
  1329	$\gamma'=\varepsilon$,
  1330	$\alpha'=\alpha$, $B'=B$,
  1331	$E'=E$, $C'=D$, $D'=C$.
  1332	From $G(r_0,c_0)=G(r_2,c_2)$
  1333	and $G(r_1,c_0)=G(r_3,c_2)$,
  1334	we have $r_3-r_1=r_2-r_0$.
  1335	From $L(r_0,c_0)=L(r_3,c_3)$
  1336	and $L(r_1,c_0)=L(r_2,c_3)$,
  1337	we have $r_3-r_0 = r_2-r_1$.
  1338	In summary, we get $r_0-r_1=r_1-r_0$,
  1339	which can only be possible if $q$ is even.
  1340	
  1341	(ii) \emph{If.} For $q$ a power of~$2$,
  1342	eight elements of $(G,L_\kappa)$ inducing $K_{4,4}$
  1343	are represented as columns of the following array:
  1344	$$
  1345	\begin{array}{r||c|c|c|c||c|c|c|c|}
  1346	            & v_{00} & v_{11}          & v_{23}      & v_{32}          & v_{01}          & v_{10}     & v_{22}   & v_{33}          \\\hline
  1347	 r          & 0      & 1+\kappa^2      & 1           & \kappa^2        & 0               & 1+\kappa^2 & 1        & \kappa^2        \\
  1348	 c          & 0      & 1+\kappa        & \kappa      & 1               & 1+\kappa        & 0          & 1        & \kappa          \\
  1349	 r+c        & 0      & \kappa+\kappa^2 & 1+\kappa    & 1+\kappa^2      & 1+\kappa        & 1+\kappa^2 & 0        & \kappa+\kappa^2 \\
  1350	 r+\kappa c & 0      & 1+\kappa        & 1+\kappa^2  & \kappa+\kappa^2 & \kappa+\kappa^2 & 1+\kappa^2 & 1+\kappa & 0               \\\hline
  1351	\end{array}
  1352	$$
  1353	
  1354	\end{proof}
  1355	
  1356	\begin{corollary}\label{cor:K44}
  1357	For every odd prime power $q\ge 7$, there exists a linear $K_{4,4}$-free pair of orthogonal latin squares. For every prime power $q\ge 4$, there exists a linear pair of orthogonal latin squares containing a $K_{4,4}$-pattern.
  1358	\end{corollary}
  1359	
  1360	\section{Conclusion}
  1361	As we see,
  1362	$K_{3,3}$-free latin squares are very rare objects,
  1363	at least for small orders.
  1364	Apart from the two known examples of order~$8$,
  1365	we have constructed one of order~$16$, and we have also found
  1366	two very symmetric $K_{3,3}$-free latin $(n-1)\times n$
  1367	rectangles, $n=5$ and~$9$ (and two such rectangles with $n=12$, with few symmetries). The generalization of such objects
  1368	to higher orders remains an open problem.
  1369	
  1370	The situation with $K_{4,4}$-patterns in pairs
  1371	of orthogonal latin squares seems to be different.
  1372	We have proved the existence of
